<p>The Republican-led U.S. Senate on Tuesday blocked a Democratic-led war powers resolution that would have barred President Trump from launching military action against Cuba without congressional approval. The chamber voted 51-47 on a procedural measure, largely along party lines, preventing the resolution from moving forward. AP reported that the Democratic legislation also would have required Trump to end the U.S. energy blockade on Cuba unless he received approval from Congress. Republicans argued that a war powers vote was not appropriate because there are no active U.S. hostilities against Cuba and Trump has not deployed troops, while Democrats said U.S. efforts to stop fuel shipments to the island already amount to military action. The vote followed previous failed Democratic attempts to require congressional authorization for Trump’s military operations involving Venezuela and Iran; Trump has said “Cuba is next” without specifying plans, and has said he believes Cuba’s government is on the verge of collapse.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Xt19TKiJlpOVIkwRMi7LWdEJVBkb5L79_-fh8uqTXK70WQj4f5d_7WQ8CJvF_UAQ8nA5Ef3bxLx3Bg4am1K4kSEaFfsqBK7c15kAn68qMcf4' alt='This photo shows the US Capitol dome and US Senate in Washington on Aug. 2, 2021.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Party split: Spanish-language reports said Republicans Susan Collins of Maine and Rand Paul of Kentucky joined Democrats in supporting the measure, while Almaghrib Today reported that Democrat John Fetterman opposed it.</li><li>Same-day agenda: Digi24 reported that Congress considered two resolutions on Tuesday, one tied to the Iran war and one aimed at possible military action against Cuba.</li><li>Iran tally: EFE-based reports said Democrats had mounted five votes seeking to prevent Trump from ordering additional attacks on Iran.</li><li>Cuba pressure: EFE-based reports said Trump has suggested government change on the island as part of the administration’s pressure on Havana.</li></ul><blockquote>If anyone were doing to the United States what we are doing to Cuba, we would definitely regard it as an act of war - Sen. Tim Kaine</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Sen. Tim Kaine: Kaine, a lead sponsor of the measure, argued that U.S. efforts to stop fuel shipments to Cuba amount to military action requiring congressional authorization. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995999/us-senate-blocks-bid-to-prevent-trump-from-military-action-against-cuba'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Sen. Rick Scott: Scott said a war powers vote was not appropriate because Trump had not deployed troops against Cuba. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995999/us-senate-blocks-bid-to-prevent-trump-from-military-action-against-cuba'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Senate Republicans: Republicans rejected Democratic accusations that Trump intended to use force against Cuba and accused Democrats of ignoring allegations of human rights violations against the government of Miguel Díaz-Canel. (<a href='https://lopezdoriga.com/internacional/senado-de-ee-uu-falla-en-intento-de-limitar-posibles-acciones-militares-de-trump-en-cuba/'>López-Dóriga Digital</a>)</li><li>Sen. Chuck Schumer: Schumer said before the vote that Republicans should act ahead of what he called an imminent catastrophe in Cuba, drawing a comparison to Democratic warnings about Trump’s war in Iran. (<a href='https://rccnoticias.com.do/senado-de-ee-uu-falla-en-limitar-acciones-militares-de-548458/'>RCC Noticias</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/29/us-senate-blocks-bid-to-stop-trump-using-military-against-cuba'>US Senate blocks bid to stop Trump using military against Cuba</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/senate-republicans-reject-attempt-to-end-trumps-blockade-of-cuba'>Senate Republicans reject attempt to end Trump&#x27;s blockade of Cuba</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1995999/us-senate-blocks-bid-to-prevent-tru